## Refactored with `async/await` + Error Handling

```js
/**
 * Fetches a single URL and returns parsed JSON.
 * Throws on network errors, non-2xx responses, or invalid JSON.
 */
async function load(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}: ${response.statusText} (${url})`);
  }

  try {
    return await response.json();
  } catch {
    throw new Error(`Invalid JSON from ${url}`);
  }
}

/**
 * Loads all URLs in parallel, preserving order.
 * Fails fast: rejects if ANY request fails.
 */
async function all(urls) {
  const results = await Promise.all(urls.map(load));
  return results;
}
```

### Usage

```js
try {
  const data = await all([
    "https://api.example.com/users",
    "https://api.example.com/posts",
    "https://api.example.com/comments",
  ]);
  console.log(data); // [usersJson, postsJson, commentsJson]
} catch (err) {
  console.error("Batch load failed:", err.message);
}
```

---

### Optional: resilient variant (collect partial results)

If you'd rather **not** fail the whole batch when one URL errors, swap `Promise.all` for `Promise.allSettled`:

```js
async function allSettled(urls) {
  const results = await Promise.allSettled(urls.map(load));

  return results.map((r, i) => {
    if (r.status === "fulfilled") return r.value;
    console.warn(`Load failed for ${urls[i]}: ${r.reason.message}`);
    return null; // or r.reason, depending on your needs
  });
}
```

